<img src="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/05/11/multimedia/11FD-nyc100-2026-KababKing-20-kcvj/11FD-nyc100-2026-KababKing-20-kcvj-mobileMasterAt3x-v3.jpg" alt="A plate of naan, two reddish-brown meat skewers with a side salad and green sauce. Yellow biryani is also on the table."></picture> <p class="g-credit svelte-1p3ybes">Janice Chung for The New York Times; Grid: Heather Willensky for The New York Times</p>  </div> <p class="g-text svelte-182b24z">In 2019, I <a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2019/04/01/dining/madhur-jaffrey-rap-video-mr-cardamom.html">interviewed</a> a young rapper known as Mr. Cardamom who had shot a <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iQVsVNPkPmE">music video</a> at this proudly scruffy Jackson Heights institution, where the attitude is as brusque as the kebabs are tender. “The service is horrendous, and it’s beautiful,” he told me. “You can make direct eye contact with someone and they will not come to your table.” That rapper is now the mayor of New York City, and if the biryani here is good enough for him, it’s good enough for you.</p> <dl class="bottom svelte-182b24z"><dd class="dd-address svelte-182b24z">73-01 37th Road, Jackson Heights</dd> <dd class="dd-website svelte-182b24z"><a href="https://kababking.com/">kababking.com</a></dd>
